  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[Chapter 6 in one breath]
  #v(3pt)
  #text(size: 10.1pt)[
    Stress is the loudest syllable, and five endings place it: -tion, -sion and -cian take the syllable before them; -ic and -ical do the same; -ity, -ify and -ogy stress three from the end; -ee, -eer, -ese and -ette stress the last; and a plain two-syllable word stresses the first syllable if it is a noun or an adjective and the second if it is a verb. That last rule is why RE-cord is a noun and re-CORD is a verb. In a sentence the content words are loud and the grammar words reduce to /ə/. English has three tunes — falling for statements, commands and wh-questions, rising for yes/no questions and unfinished lists, and fall-rise for doubt — and the tune is chosen by what the question expects, not by its punctuation. Two words rhyme when everything from the stressed vowel onward is identical, which is why night rhymes with light and never with eight.
  ]
]

